| ERG-037 Electromagnetism | [Entrance](Electromagnetism.lingua), 134 authored Records including 28 source Records, plus eleven reused learning Records and a Physics entrance | Syntax; five-entry discovery of the 346-document composition; exact 116-Record learning selection/reachability; four composed routes, external URLs, internal learning locations and three original programs preserved after relocated export; all ten earlier publication tests; independent exact electrostatics, scattering recursion, discrete Fourier, continuum convergence and teaching checks |

**Historical authored-completion evidence, 28–30 October 2026.**

Electromagnetism checks on 29 October 2026: the new publication test passed after adding ordinary index links around shared phasor and detector steps; all ten earlier subject tests passed in the accompanying regression run. `cargo check --locked -p lince-resources --features publication --tests` passed. The subject starts with familiar observations, charge/field/potential/current, geometry, units and explicit mathematical bridges; it deepens through electrostatic boundaries/Green functions/multipoles/energy, electric and magnetic matter, conduction/circuits, induction and electromechanics, Maxwell conservation, gauge/action and covariant relativity, waves/interfaces/dispersion/causality, lines/guides/cavities, radiation/antennas/scattering, material measurements, inverse reasoning and computational validation.

Sustained reading includes all OpenStax Volume 2 electricity/magnetism chapters, both complete open Ellingson volumes, the full current Tong and Fitzpatrick texts and vector-calculus course, a complete Staelin or Haus–Melcher engineering companion, substantial Feynman comparison, Portuguese UFJF/IFRN and optional UFRGS/UFRJ material, the complete public advanced-undergraduate and available graduate MIT programmes, complete Schneider FDTD and NIST TN 1536 methods, full PML/model/inverse papers and precise primary manuals. Griffiths and Jackson are clearly optional commercial companions. Source Records preserve actual editions, sign/unit conventions, component rights, free-reading versus open-licence distinctions, title-page/catalogue differences, available-resource limits and versions not completely inspected. External books, PDFs, articles, media, datasets and software remain links.

Sixteen substantial advanced investigations cover static boundary/Green/multipole methods; dielectric and magnetic response; field/circuit/probe interfaces; force, stress and energy; relativistic/action methods; dispersive pulses; guides/lines/cavities; radiation/antennas; scattering/extinction; static and time-domain verification/PML; inverse reconstruction; experimental methods; microscopic/macroscopic comparison; and infrastructure interfaces. They rejoin an integrated electromagnetic system, independent scholarship and demanding completion. Their requirements describe future reader work; no claim is made that physical experiments, a commissioned device or independent thesis results were already performed.

Three original inline programs ran in fresh Python processes from another working directory. Conservative electrostatics matched an independent exact-Fraction dense Gaussian solution, interface/energy/charge identities, reversal/scaling and the analytical discrete sinusoidal mode in 257 cases, including second-order continuum refinement. The lossless layered-wave transfer calculation matched independent Fresnel scattering recursion in 352 cases, with energy, phase, index-matched/zero/half-wave limits, reverse reciprocity and scale checks. The staggered periodic Yee calculation matched independent exact discrete Fourier fields in 684 cases, including uniform/limiting modes, its actual modified-energy invariant, mean conservation and second-order fixed-time continuum convergence. Ninety-four invalid domains were rejected and twelve exact/independent teaching calculations passed. The original examples explicitly declare normalized units and physical/model limits; their correctness is not a claim about an untested general solver.

